quick_facts: Records cumulative personal radiation dose
quick_facts: Passive types: film badge, TLD, OSL — read monthly or quarterly
quick_facts: Electronic types show real-time dose and dose-rate with alarms
quick_facts: Legally required for classified radiation workers
quick_facts: Worn on the torso; special versions for fingers (ring badges)

sources:
    {"organization":"NRC","title":"Occupational Radiation Protection","url":"https://www.nrc.gov/about-nrc/radiation/protects-you/protection-principles.html"}

definition:
    A device worn by radiation workers to record the cumulative ionizing-radiation dose they receive. Passive types — film badges, thermoluminescent (TLD), and optically stimulated luminescent (OSL) dosimeters — are read out periodically, typically monthly. Direct-reading electronic dosimeters show live dose and dose-rate with alarms. Legal limits make them mandatory for classified workers in medicine, nuclear power, research, and industry.

debunking_snippet:
    “A Geiger counter and a dosimeter are the same thing.” They're not — a Geiger counter detects radiation instantly but forgets; a dosimeter remembers your accumulated dose over weeks and months.
